The Debye-Hückel-Onsager equation for a strong electrolyte is:
\lambda_m = \lambda_m^0 - b\sqrt{c}
Where:
• \(\lambda_m\) is the molar conductivity, with unit \(S cm^2 mol^{-1}\).
• \(\lambda_m^0\) is the limiting molar conductivity, with unit \(S cm^2 mol^{-1}\).
• \(c\) is the concentration, with unit \(mol L^{-1}\).
• \(b\) is the slope.
For the equation to be dimensionally consistent, the unit of \(b\sqrt{c}\) must be the same as the unit of \(\lambda_m\).
\text{unit of } b\sqrt{c} = \text{unit of } \lambda_m
First, let's find the unit of \(\sqrt{c}\):
\text{unit of } \sqrt{c} = \sqrt{mol L^{-1}} = (mol L^{-1})^{1/2} = mol^{1/2} L^{-1/2}
Now, we can find the unit of \(b\):
\text{unit of } b = \frac{\text{unit of } \lambda_m}{\text{unit of } \sqrt{c}}
\text{unit of } b = \frac{S cm^2 mol^{-1}}{mol^{1/2} L^{-1/2}}
\text{unit of } b = S cm^2 mol^{-1} mol^{-1/2} L^{1/2}
\text{unit of } b = S cm^2 mol^{-3/2} L^{1/2}

