Resistivity (\(\rho\)) is given as \(5 \times 10^{-3} \Omega \text{cm}\).
Conductivity (K) is the reciprocal of resistivity.
K = \frac{1}{\rho} = \frac{1}{5 \times 10^{-3}} \Omega^{-1} \text{cm}^{-1}

### Step 2: Calculate molar conductivity (\(\Lambda_m\))

The molarity (M) of the solution is 0.8 M.
The formula for molar conductivity is:
\Lambda_m = K \times \frac{1000}{M}
Substitute the values of K and M:
\Lambda_m = \frac{1}{5 \times 10^{-3}} \times \frac{1000}{0.8}
Simplify the expression:
\Lambda_m = \frac{1000}{5 \times 10^{-3} \times 0.8} = \frac{1000}{4 \times 10^{-3}}
\Lambda_m = \frac{1000}{0.004} = 250000 \Omega^{-1} \text{cm}^{2} \text{mol}^{-1}
To express this in the required format (\( \times 10^{4} \Omega^{-1} \text{cm}^{2} \text{mol}^{-1} \)): 
\Lambda_m = 25 \times 10^{4} \Omega^{-1} \text{cm}^{2} \text{mol}^{-1}
The nearest integer value to be filled is 25.

### Step 1: Calculate Molarity (M) of NaOH solution

Given: Concentration of NaOH = 0.2% (w/v)
Therefore, 0.2 g of NaOH is present in 100 ml of solution.
Molarity of NaOH solution is calculated as:
\text{Molarity} = \frac{\text{moles of solute}}{V_{\text{ml}}} \times 1000
= \frac{0.2/40}{100} \times 1000 = \frac{0.2}{40 \times 100} \times 1000 = \frac{2}{40} \text{ M}

### Step 2: Convert resistivity to appropriate units and calculate conductivity (K)

