questions.question:
The electrode potential of the following half cell at 298 K \(X|X^{2+}(0.001M) \| Y^{2+}(0.01M)|Y\) is _________ \(\times 10^{-2}\) V (Nearest integer).
Given:
E^{0}_{X^{2+}|X} = -2.36 \text{ V}
E^{0}_{Y^{2+}|Y} = +0.36 \text{ V}
\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.06 \text{ V}

questions.explanation:
The overall reaction is:
X + Y^{2+} \rightarrow Y + X^{2+}
Standard cell potential \(E^{0}_{Cell}\) is calculated as:
E^{0}_{Cell} = E^{0}_{Cathode} - E^{0}_{Anode}
Here, Y is reduced (cathode) and X is oxidized (anode).
E^{0}_{Cell} = 0.36 - (-2.36) = 2.72 \text{ V}
Using the Nernst equation for the cell potential \(E_{Cell}\):
E_{Cell} = E^{0}_{Cell} - \frac{2.303RT}{nF} \log Q
Given \(\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.06\) V and \(n=2\) (since \(X \rightarrow X^{2+}\) and \(Y^{2+} \rightarrow Y\)).
The reaction quotient \(Q\) is \(Q = \frac{[X^{2+}]}{[Y^{2+}]}\).
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - \frac{0.06}{2} \log \frac{[X^{2+}]}{[Y^{2+}]}
Substitute the given concentrations:
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - \frac{0.06}{2} \log \frac{0.001}{0.01}
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - 0.03 \log (0.1)
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - 0.03 (-1)
E_{Cell} = 2.72 + 0.03 = 2.75 \text{ V}
To express the answer in the format \(\times 10^{-2}\) V:
E_{Cell} = 2.75 \text{ V} = 275 \times 10^{-2} \text{ V}
The nearest integer is 275.

questions.question:
Reduction potential of ions are given below:
\begin{matrix} ClO_{4}^{-} & IO_{4}^{-} & BrO_{4}^{-} \\ E^{\circ} = 1.19 \text{ V} & E^{\circ} = 1.65 \text{ V} & E^{\circ} = 1.74 \text{ V} \end{matrix}
The correct order of their oxidizing power is.

questions.explanation:
The oxidizing power of a species is directly proportional to its standard reduction potential (E°). A higher positive value of E° indicates a stronger tendency to get reduced, and thus, a stronger oxidizing agent.
\text{Oxidizing power} \propto \text{Reduction potential}
The given standard reduction potentials are:
E^{\circ}(ClO_{4}^{-}) = 1.19 \text{ V}
E^{\circ}(IO_{4}^{-}) = 1.65 \text{ V}
E^{\circ}(BrO_{4}^{-}) = 1.74 \text{ V}
Arranging these values in increasing order:
1.19 \text{ V} < 1.65 \text{ V} < 1.74 \text{ V}
Therefore, the order of standard reduction potentials is:
E^{\circ}(ClO_{4}^{-}) < E^{\circ}(IO_{4}^{-}) < E^{\circ}(BrO_{4}^{-})
Since oxidizing power follows the same trend, the order of oxidizing power is:
ClO_{4}^{-} < IO_{4}^{-} < BrO_{4}^{-}
Writing this in decreasing order gives:
BrO_{4}^{-} > IO_{4}^{-} > ClO_{4}^{-}

### Step 1: Calculate the volume of nickel required

\text{Volume of nickel required} = 100 \text{ cm}^2 \times 0.001 \text{ mm} \times \frac{1 \text{ cm}}{10 \text{ mm}}
= 100 \times 0.001 \times 0.1 \text{ cm}^3
= 0.01 \text{ cm}^3

### Step 2: Calculate the mass of nickel required

Given density of Nickel (\(\rho_{\text{Ni}}\)) = \(10 \text{g mL}^{-1}\) = \(10 \text{g cm}^{-3}\) (since \(1 \text{mL} = 1 \text{cm}^3\))
\text{Mass of Nickel required} = \text{Volume} \times \text{Density}
= 0.01 \text{ cm}^3 \times 10 \text{ g cm}^{-3}
= 0.1 \text{ gm}

### Step 3: Calculate the moles of nickel required

Molar mass of Nickel = \(60 \text{ g mol}^{-1}\)
\text{Moles of Nickel} = \frac{\text{Mass}}{\text{Molar mass}} = \frac{0.1 \text{ gm}}{60 \text{ g mol}^{-1}}
= \frac{1}{600} \text{ mol}

### Step 4: Determine the charge required for electrodeposition

The reduction reaction for nickel is:
\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2\text{e}^- \to \text{Ni(s)}
From the reaction, \(2\) moles of electrons are required to deposit \(1\) mole of Nickel.
Charge required for coating \(1\) mol Ni = \(2 \times F = 2 \times 96500 \text{ C}\)
Charge required for coating \(\frac{1}{600}\) mol Ni = \(2 \times 96500 \times \frac{1}{600} \text{ C}\)
= \frac{193000}{600} \text{ C} = \frac{1930}{6} \text{ C} = \frac{965}{3} \text{ C}

### Step 5: Calculate the time 'x'

We know that current \(I = \frac{q}{t}\), where \(q\) is the charge and \(t\) is the time.
Given current \(I = 2\text{A}\).
t = \frac{q}{I} = \frac{965/3 \text{ C}}{2 \text{ A}}
t = \frac{965}{3 \times 2} \text{ s} = \frac{965}{6} \text{ s}
t \approx 160.83 \text{ s}
Rounding to the nearest integer, \(x \approx 161\) seconds.

### Step 1: Calculate molar conductivity (\(\Lambda_{m}\))

The formula for molar conductivity is:
\Lambda_{m} = \frac{k}{C} \times 1000
Given specific conductance \(k = 5 \times 10^{-5}\) S \(cm^{-1}\) and concentration \(C = 0.0025\) M.
\Lambda_{m} = \frac{5 \times 10^{-5} \times 10^{3}}{0.0025} = \frac{5 \times 10^{-2}}{2.5 \times 10^{-3}}
\Lambda_{m} = 20 S cm^{2} mol^{-1}

### Step 2: Calculate degree of dissociation (\(\alpha\))

The degree of dissociation \(\alpha\) is given by the ratio of molar conductivity to limiting molar conductivity:
\alpha = \frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{m}^{0}}
Given limiting molar conductivity \(\Lambda_{m}^{0} = 400\) S \(cm^{2}\) \(mol^{-1}\).
\alpha = \frac{20}{400} = \frac{1}{20}

### Step 3: Calculate dissociation constant (\(K_{a}\))

For a weak acid, the dissociation constant \(K_{a}\) is given by Ostwald's dilution law:
K_{a} = \frac{C\alpha^{2}}{1-\alpha}
Substitute the values of \(C\) and \(\alpha\):
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025 \times (\frac{1}{20})^{2}}{1 - \frac{1}{20}}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025 \times \frac{1}{400}}{\frac{19}{20}}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025}{400} \times \frac{20}{19}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025}{20 \times 19} = \frac{0.0025}{380}
K_{a} \approx 6.5789 \times 10^{-6}
To express this in the format \(\times 10^{-7}\):
K_{a} = 65.789 \times 10^{-7}
Rounding to the nearest integer, the dissociation constant is \(66 \times 10^{-7}\).

questions.question:
A volume of x mL of 5 M \(NaHCO_{3}\) solution was mixed with 10 mL of 2 M \(H_{2}CO_{3}\) solution to make an electrolytic buffer. If the same buffer was used in the following electrochemical cell to record a cell potential of 235.3 mV, then the value of x = ______ mL (nearest integer).
The electrochemical cell is: \(Sn(s) | Sn(OH)_{6}^{2-} (0.5 \text{ M}) | HSnO_{2}^{-} (0.05 \text{ M}) | OH^{-} (aq) | Bi_{2}O_{3}(s) | Bi(s)\)
Consider upto one place of decimal for intermediate calculations.
Given :
E^{\circ}_{[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}/HSnO_{2}^{-}} = -0.9 \text{ V}
E^{\circ}_{Bi_{2}O_{3}/Bi} = -0.44 \text{ V}
pK_{a_{(H_{2}CO_{3})}} = 6.11
\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059 \text{ V}
\text{Antilog}(1.29) = 19.5

questions.explanation:
We have considered the cell:
Pt|HSnO_{2}^{-}(aq), [Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}(aq), OH^{-}(aq)|Bi_{2}O_{3}(s)|Bi(s)
[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-} = 0.5 \text{ M}
[HSnO_{2}^{-}] = 0.05 \text{ M}
E^{\circ}_{cell} = E^{\circ}_{cathode} - E^{\circ}_{anode}
E^{\circ}_{cell} = E^{\circ}_{Bi_{2}O_{3}/Bi} - E^{\circ}_{HSnO_{2}^{-}/[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}}
E^{\circ}_{cell} = -0.44 - (-0.9) = +0.46 \text{ V}

### Oxidation Half :

HSnO_{2}^{-} + 3OH^{-} \rightarrow [Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-} + 2e^{-}

### Reduction Half :

Bi_{2}O_{3} + 3H_{2}O + 6e^{-} \rightarrow 2Bi + 6OH^{-}
To balance electrons, multiply oxidation half by 3:
3HSnO_{2}^{-}(aq) + Bi_{2}O_{3}(s) + 6H_{2}O + 3OH^{-}(aq) \rightarrow 3[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}(aq) + 2Bi(s)
Number of electrons transferred, \(n = 6\).
Using Nernst Equation:
E_{cell} = E^{\circ}_{cell} - \frac{0.059}{n} \log Q
Q = \frac{([Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-})^{3}}{([HSnO_{2}^{-}])^{3} [OH^{-}]^{3}}
0.2353 = 0.46 - \frac{0.059}{6} \log \frac{(0.5)^{3}}{(0.05)^{3} [OH^{-}]^{3}}
0.2353 = 0.46 - \frac{0.059}{6} \log \left[ \frac{10^{3}}{[OH^{-}]^{3}} \right]
0.2353 = 0.46 - \frac{0.059}{6} \times 3 \log \left[ \frac{10}{[OH^{-}]} \right]
0.2353 = 0.46 - \frac{0.059}{2} \log \left[ \frac{10}{[OH^{-}]} \right]
\frac{0.059}{2} \log \left[ \frac{10}{[OH^{-}]} \right] = 0.46 - 0.2353 = 0.2247
\log \left[ \frac{10}{[OH^{-}]} \right] = \frac{2 \times 0.2247}{0.059} = \frac{0.4494}{0.059} \approx 7.617
\log \left[ \frac{10}{[OH^{-}]} \right] = 7.6
1 - \log [OH^{-}] = 7.6
1 + pOH = 7.6
pOH = 6.6
pH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 6.6 = 7.4
For the buffer solution, using Henderson-Hasselbalch equation:
pH = pK_{a_{1}} + \log \frac{[HCO_{3}^{-}]}{[H_{2}CO_{3}]}
7.4 = 6.11 + \log \frac{5x}{2 \times 10}
7.4 = 6.11 + \log \frac{5x}{20}
7.4 - 6.11 = \log \frac{x}{4}
1.29 = \log \frac{x}{4}
\frac{x}{4} = \text{Antilog}(1.29)
\frac{x}{4} = 19.5
x = 19.5 \times 4
x = 78
The value of x is 78 mL (nearest integer).
Note : In question paper, \(E^{\circ}_{HSnO_{2}^{-}/[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}} = -0.9 \text{ V}\) data is given, but NTA has given answer by considering \(E^{\circ}_{[Sn(OH)_{6}]^{2-}/HSnO_{2}^{-}} = -0.9 \text{ V}\) therefore this question should be BONUS.

