### Step 1: Write the half-cell reactions and overall cell reaction.

\text{Anode} \Rightarrow \frac{1}{2} H_{2}(g) \rightarrow H^{+}(aq) + e^{-}
\text{Cathode} \Rightarrow Fe^{3+} + e^{-} \rightarrow Fe^{2+}
\text{Overall} \frac{1}{2} H_{2} + Fe^{3+} \rightarrow H^{+} + Fe^{2+}

### Step 2: Calculate the standard cell potential (E°_cell).

The standard cell potential is given by:
E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{anode}}
E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}} - E^{\circ}_{H^{+}/1/2H_{2}}
E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0.771 \text{ V} - 0 \text{ V} = 0.771 \text{ V}

### Step 3: Apply the Nernst equation.

The Nernst equation for the cell reaction is:
E_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.059}{n} \log Q
Where \(n\) is the number of electrons transferred (here \(n=1\)) and \(Q\) is the reaction quotient.
Q = \frac{[Fe^{2+}][H^{+}]}{[Fe^{3+}][P_{H_{2}}]^{1/2}}
Given \(E_{\text{cell}} = 0.712 \text{ V}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0.771 \text{ V}\), \([H^{+}] = 1\), and \(P_{H_{2}} = 1 \text{ atm}\).
0.712 = 0.771 - \frac{0.059}{1} \log \frac{[Fe^{2+}](1)}{[Fe^{3+}](1)^{1/2}}
0.712 = 0.771 - 0.059 \log \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]}

### Step 4: Solve for the ratio \([Fe^{2+}]/[Fe^{3+}]\).

0.059 \log \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = 0.771 - 0.712
0.059 \log \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = 0.059
\log \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = \frac{0.059}{0.059}
\log \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = 1
\frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = 10^{1}
\text{So} \frac{[Fe^{2+}]}{[Fe^{3+}]} = 10

