### Detailed Solution


### Step 1: Identify the reaction and relevant formula

Aluminium chloride (\(AlCl_{3}\)) dissociates into \(Al^{3+}\) and \(Cl^{-}\) ions. At the cathode, \(Al^{3+}\) ions are reduced to Al metal:
Al^{3+} + 3e^{-} \to Al
The number of gram equivalents of a substance deposited during electrolysis is given by Faraday's law:
\text{Gram equivalents} = \frac{It}{F}
Where I is the current, t is the time in seconds, and F is Faraday's constant (96500 C \(mol^{-1}\)).

### Step 2: Calculate the mass of aluminium deposited

The number of gram equivalents can also be expressed as \(\frac{\text{mass (w)}}{\text{equivalent weight}}\). For aluminium, the equivalent weight is \(\frac{\text{molar mass}}{\text{valency}} = \frac{27}{3} = 9\).
So, \(\frac{w}{27} \times 3\) represents the gram equivalents of Al deposited.
Given:
• Current (I) = 2 A
• Time (t) = 30 minutes = \(30 \times 60 = 1800\) seconds
Equating the two expressions for gram equivalents:
\frac{w}{27} \times 3 = \frac{I \times t}{F}
Substitute the given values:
\frac{w}{27} \times 3 = \frac{2 \times (30 \times 60)}{96500}
\frac{w}{27} \times 3 = \frac{3600}{96500}
w = \frac{3600 \times 27}{96500 \times 3}
w = \frac{3600 \times 9}{96500}
w = \frac{32400}{96500}
w \approx 0.33575 \text{ g}
Rounding to three significant figures, the mass of aluminium deposited is \(0.336\) g.

### Given Data

Concentration \(C = 0.0015\) M
Conductance \(G = 5 \times 10^{-7}\) S
Length of electrodes \(\ell = 120\) cm
Area of cross section \(a = 1\) \(cm^{2}\)

### Step 1: Calculate conductivity (\(\kappa\))

G = \kappa \times \frac{a}{\ell}
5 \times 10^{-7} = \kappa \times \frac{1}{120}
\kappa = 6 \times 10^{-5}\ S\ cm^{-1}

### Step 2: Calculate degree of dissociation (\(\alpha\))

Given \(pH = 4\)
[H^{+}] = 10^{-4}\ M
For a weak monobasic acid, \([H^{+}] = C\alpha\)
10^{-4} = 0.0015\alpha
\alpha = \frac{10^{-4}}{0.0015}

### Step 3: Calculate limiting molar conductivity (\(\Lambda_{m}^{0}\))

Molar conductivity (\(\Lambda_{m}^{c}\)) is given by:
\Lambda_{m}^{c} = \frac{\kappa \times 1000}{C}
Also, the degree of dissociation is related to molar conductivities by:
\alpha = \frac{\Lambda_{m}^{c}}{\Lambda_{m}^{0}}
Substitute the expression for \(\Lambda_{m}^{c}\) into the equation for \(\alpha\):
\alpha = \frac{\frac{\kappa \times 1000}{C}}{\Lambda_{m}^{0}}
Now, substitute the values of \(\alpha\), \(\kappa\), and \(C\):
\frac{10^{-4}}{0.0015} = \frac{\frac{6 \times 10^{-5} \times 1000}{0.0015}}{\Lambda_{m}^{0}}
Rearranging to solve for \(\Lambda_{m}^{0}\):
\Lambda_{m}^{0} = \frac{6 \times 10^{-5} \times 1000}{10^{-4}}
\Lambda_{m}^{0} = \frac{6 \times 10^{-2}}{10^{-4}}
\Lambda_{m}^{0} = 6 \times 10^{2}\ S\ cm^{2}\ mol^{-1}

### Step 4: Determine the value of Z

The problem states that the limiting molar conductivity is \(Z\times 10^{2}\) S \(cm^{2}\) \(mol^{-1}\).
Comparing our calculated value with the given expression:
Z \times 10^{2}\ S\ cm^{2}\ mol^{-1} = 6 \times 10^{2}\ S\ cm^{2}\ mol^{-1}
Z = 6

