For weak acid, \(\alpha = \frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{0}}\) 
K_{a} = \frac{C\alpha^{2}}{1-\alpha} \implies K_{a}(1-\alpha) = C\alpha^{2}
\implies K_{a}\left(1-\frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{0}}\right) = C\left(\frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{0}}\right)^{2}
\implies K_{a} - \frac{\Lambda_{m}K_{a}}{\Lambda_{0}} = \frac{C\Lambda_{m}^{2}}{(\Lambda_{0})^{2}}
Divide by '\(\Lambda_{m}\)'
\implies \frac{K_{a}}{\Lambda_{m}} = \frac{C\Lambda_{m}}{(\Lambda_{0})^{2}} + \frac{K_{a}}{\Lambda_{0}}
\implies \frac{1}{\Lambda_{m}} = \frac{C\Lambda_{m}}{K_{a}(\Lambda_{0})^{2}} + \frac{1}{\Lambda_{0}}
Plot \(\frac{1}{\Lambda_{m}}\) vs \(C\Lambda_{m}\) has
\text{Slope} = \frac{1}{K_{a}(\Lambda_{0})^{2}} = S
\text{y-intercept} = \frac{1}{\Lambda_{0}} = P
Then, \(\frac{P}{S} = \frac{\frac{1}{\Lambda_{0}}}{\frac{1}{K_{a}(\Lambda_{0})^{2}}} = K_{a}\Lambda_{0}\)
